A **custom property** is a CSS property whose name starts with `--`. You read it with `var(--name, fallback)`. Because it is a real part of the cascade, its value is resolved in the browser, can differ per element and can change at runtime.

## Tokens and theming

Design tokens are named design decisions (colours, spacing, radii, type scale). A common two-layer approach:

```css
:root {
  /* primitive tokens */
  --blue-600: #2563eb;
  --gray-900: #111827;
  --gray-50: #f9fafb;
  --space-2: 0.5rem;

  /* semantic tokens */
  --color-bg: var(--gray-50);
  --color-text: var(--gray-900);
  --color-accent: var(--blue-600);
}

:root[data-theme="dark"] {
  --color-bg: #0b1020;
  --color-text: #e5e7eb;
}

.button {
  background: var(--color-accent);
  padding: var(--space-2) calc(var(--space-2) * 2);
  color: var(--color-bg);
}
```

Components use only semantic tokens, so a theme is one scope of overrides. You can also respond to `@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark)`.

## Runtime vs preprocessor variables

| | Sass/Less variable | CSS custom property |
|---|---|---|
| Resolved | at build time | in the browser |
| Cascade and inheritance | no | yes |
| Change per element or media query | no (new compiled rule) | yes |
| Readable from JavaScript | no | yes, via `getComputedStyle` and `style.setProperty` |
| Usable in `calc()` and math | only before compile | yes, at runtime |

## Inheritance and scoping

Custom properties inherit by default, so setting `--card-padding` on a section affects every descendant that uses it. This enables component-level APIs: a `.card` reads `var(--card-padding, 1rem)` and consumers override it.

## Caveats

- An invalid value is only detected at use time, then the property falls back to its initial or inherited value ("invalid at computed-value time"). Registering with `@property` adds a type, an initial value and animation support.
- Variables cannot be used in media query conditions.
- Overusing deep alias chains makes debugging harder; keep two or three layers.
- Frequently changing a variable on a high ancestor can trigger a large style recalculation, so scope fast-changing values (for example a drag position) to the smallest element.

## Approach

1. **Start with the content and a narrow single column.** Base styles describe mobile; enhancements are layered on with `min-width` queries, so small devices download and parse less override CSS.
2. **Set breakpoints where the design breaks**, not at named device sizes. Stretch the viewport until lines get too long or items look sparse, then add a query there. Having three or four breakpoints expressed as tokens is typical.
3. **Prefer intrinsic layouts first**: grid with `auto-fit`, flex wrapping and `clamp()` often need no query at all.
4. **Use container queries for reusable components** so a card adapts to the space it is given, not the viewport.
5. **Always include the viewport meta tag**: `<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">`.

```css
.layout {
  display: grid;
  gap: 1rem;
  padding: 1rem;
}
@media (min-width: 48rem) {      /* content now supports a sidebar */
  .layout { grid-template-columns: 220px 1fr; }
}
@media (min-width: 80rem) {
  .layout { grid-template-columns: 260px 1fr 300px; max-width: 90rem; margin-inline: auto; }
}

img { max-width: 100%; height: auto; }

.card-list { container-type: inline-size; }
@container (min-width: 30rem) {
  .card { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 8rem 1fr; }
}
```

## Navigation and interaction

- Collapse navigation into a menu on small screens; keep touch targets reasonably large (commonly around 44px).
- Do not rely on hover for essential actions, because touch devices lack it.
- Use responsive images (`srcset`, `sizes`) and lazy loading.

## Tables on small screens

- **Scroll wrapper:** wrap in `overflow-x: auto` and keep the key column sticky; simple and keeps data intact.
- **Stacked cards:** below a breakpoint, switch rows to blocks and show column labels using `data-label` attributes. This is more readable but needs more CSS, and you must keep table semantics accessible.
- **Prioritise columns:** hide or collapse secondary columns behind a details toggle.

```css
.table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; }
.table-wrap table { min-width: 40rem; }
```

## Common mistakes

- Desktop-first CSS with many `max-width` overrides.
- Fixed pixel widths and heights on containers.
- Testing only by resizing a desktop browser, without real devices, zoom or text enlargement.

The main problems at scale are **global namespace collisions, specificity wars, dead code nobody dares delete and inconsistent visual decisions**. Structure should address each one.

## 1. Tokens as the foundation

Colours, spacing, typography and radii live as custom properties (see the design tokens question). Components consume tokens, so a rebrand or dark theme does not touch component files.

## 2. Scope component styles

| Option | Idea | Trade-off |
|---|---|---|
| CSS Modules | class names are hashed per file at build time | plain CSS, zero runtime; sharing across files needs explicit composition |
| CSS-in-JS (runtime) | styles co-located in components | flexible theming, but runtime cost and tooling concerns, particularly with server rendering |
| Zero-runtime CSS-in-JS / utility frameworks | extracted static CSS | needs tooling or a team convention |
| BEM naming | convention only | no tooling, but relies on discipline |

```css
/* Button.module.css */
.root { padding: var(--space-2) var(--space-4); background: var(--color-accent); }
.primary { font-weight: 600; }
```

```jsx
import styles from "./Button.module.css";
export const Button = ({ children }) => (
  <button className={`${styles.root} ${styles.primary}`}>{children}</button>
);
```

## 3. Control specificity and order

Keep selectors at one class where possible, avoid IDs, deep descendant chains and `!important`. Use **cascade layers** so ordering is explicit and independent of specificity or file order:

```css
@layer reset, tokens, base, components, utilities;

@layer components {
  .card { padding: 1rem; }
}
@layer utilities {
  .p-0 { padding: 0; }  /* wins over .card regardless of selector weight */
}
```

Unlayered styles beat layered ones, which is useful for third-party overrides but easy to forget.

## 4. Organisation

- A small global layer: reset, tokens, base elements, a few utilities.
- Component styles next to the component, so deleting a component deletes its CSS.
- Shared primitives (Stack, Grid, Text) in a design-system package with documented props.

## 5. Guardrails

- Stylelint rules for specificity, naming and forbidden patterns; Prettier formatting.
- Visual regression tests or a component catalogue such as Storybook.
- Code review checklist: tokens used, no magic numbers, responsive behaviour checked.

## Common mistakes

- Mixing several styling approaches without a reason.
- Overriding library styles using increasingly specific selectors instead of layers or documented theming hooks.
- Letting a global stylesheet grow because nothing is safe to delete.

Treat the dashboard as three levels: the **shell**, the **widget grid** and the **widget internals**. Use grid for the first two and flexbox for the third.

## 1. Shell (grid)

```css
.app {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 240px minmax(0, 1fr);
  grid-template-rows: auto minmax(0, 1fr);
  grid-template-areas:
    "sidebar header"
    "sidebar main";
  height: 100dvh;
}
.sidebar { grid-area: sidebar; overflow-y: auto; }
.header  { grid-area: header; }
.main    { grid-area: main; overflow: auto; padding: 1rem; }
```

## Why `minmax(0, 1fr)` and not `1fr`

`1fr` is shorthand for `minmax(auto, 1fr)`. The `auto` minimum means a track will not shrink below the minimum content size of its items, so a wide table or long unbroken string can stretch the column and cause overflow. `minmax(0, 1fr)` allows the track to shrink so the item can scroll or truncate instead. The same applies to `minmax(0, 1fr)` rows when you want the main area to scroll internally.

## 2. Widget grid (auto-fit)

```css
.widgets {
  display: grid;
  gap: 1rem;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 18rem), 1fr));
}
.widget--wide { grid-column: span 2; }
@media (max-width: 40rem) { .widget--wide { grid-column: auto; } }
```

Each card is at least 18rem and the browser fits as many as it can, with no media query. `auto-fit` collapses empty tracks, so the remaining cards stretch to fill the row; `auto-fill` keeps empty tracks, so cards stay at a consistent size. The `min(100%, 18rem)` guard avoids overflow on very narrow containers.

## 3. Widget internals (flexbox)

```css
.widget { display: flex; flex-direction: column; min-width: 0; }
.widget__header { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: .5rem; }
.widget__title { flex: 1; min-width: 0; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis; white-space: nowrap; }
.widget__body { flex: 1; overflow: auto; }
```

`flex: 1` on the body pushes a footer to the bottom, and `min-width: 0` lets the title truncate instead of forcing the card wider.

## Responsive shell

Below a chosen breakpoint, collapse the sidebar into an off-canvas drawer, or switch the shell to a single column. Use `grid-template-areas` redefinitions inside the media query.

## Common mistakes

- Using `1fr` columns with tables inside, then wondering why the page scrolls horizontally.
- Forgetting a height constraint so nested scrolling regions do not work.
- Using absolute positioning for placement that grid areas handle more cleanly.
- Reordering visually without considering keyboard order.
